A||||15||mrfixit58||HELP!!! My rear is sagging!||||5||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||08-29-2000||04:01 PM||||Now that I have your attention  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I noticed a gap between the tail lights and the rear facia on my 87 GT. I also noticed that the body lines from the facia don't line up with the side marker lenses. Is it a big geal to adjust the facia to remove this gap?<P>Thanks for the help in advance,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula||08-29-2000||04:51 PM||||all rear ends have a crack in them....||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||08-29-2000||07:11 PM||||Yea, I deserved that. And, the older they get the lower they sag  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> .<P>But, I guess the real question I need to ask is... Is there any adjustment behind the rear bumber cover that will raise the cover about 1/4 inch? If so, what do I need to know that will help me remove and re-install the rear cover?<P>Thanks,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000003||Standard||08-29-2000||07:36 PM||||I have the same problem with my 86 GT rear fascia.. an ugly 1/2 inch gap between the molding and the tail-lights.  I pulled off the fascia, but there doesn't seem to be any way to pull it up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">... it might be time for a new fascia..<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">   <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula||08-29-2000||07:45 PM||||I dont know, Im sure someone here knows?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wolfhound||08-29-2000||09:10 PM||||You might try removing the taillight lens and checking the plastic retainers underneath. some may be missing or broken. Also check lower retainers. It,s also possible that the conection points on the underbody are distorted from a previous accident.<BR> At any rate, removing the rear facia should reveal the problem. There is very little work involved in doing this.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||08-30-2000||07:28 AM||||Thanks guys. I'll give it a shot.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000007||Phil||08-30-2000||10:45 AM||||Was talking to Ed Parks in Louisville and he said that he had a guy near him that puts facias and fenders in a controlled heat situation and bingo they return to their original shape. Check with your local body shops and see if anybody in your area does the same thing.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Thor||08-30-2000||03:17 PM||||If Anybody notices other than you, instead of telling them It's a design flaw, tell them It's a ventillation design feature!||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierocrazy||08-30-2000||05:09 PM||||i dont know either , but I am going to disassemble the whole car an paint it peice by peice so all those body gaps better have an ajustment, there has to be a way<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||08-31-2000||05:48 AM||||Another MN member!! Welcome Thor!<P>Like I said, I couldn't find any way to raise the fascia.  It's just held in by those plastic push rivit thingies all along the back.. There's a little bit of adjustment on the side where the marker lights screw in, but not enough to pull the whole thing up.. <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">   <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||08-31-2000||03:21 PM||||I went to the bone yard today and got to take a look at a 86 GT with a damaged left rear corner. I looks like the top of the rear bumper cover attaches to a stamped metal bracket that's bolted to the rear bumper frame. I may be able to find a little adjustment in this bracket. Does this ring a bell with anyone?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000012||Zorba||08-31-2000||03:56 PM||||Jenny Craig might know? ( <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wolfhound||09-01-2000||08:20 AM||||Roy , You are correct. The top of the facia attaches to the stamped plate the shock grid is attached to. You have adjustment.<BR>Coy||||reg||14
Z||000014||mrfixit58||09-01-2000||03:01 PM||||Wolfhound,<P>Thanks for the confirm. Just to make sure I rechecked a different car today while I was back a the yard. Looks like I've got another project!<P>Thanks again,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>P.S. Wolfhound... how's the cabin coming along?||||reg||14
Z||000015||Wolfhound||09-02-2000||07:42 AM||||Roy, Slow, I,ve had a lot of work this summer and the cleaning and perp on the 150+ year old logs is ongoing. I have plans worked out in some detail now. Hope to come out of the ground with it soon. When I do I,ll post some photos in the O/T section.<BR>Coy||||reg||1
